L3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Sound\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:UALM: Unified Audio Language Model for Understanding, Generation and Reasoning\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Jinchuan Tian , cite6†Sang-gil Lee , cite7†Zhifeng Kong , cite8†Sreyan Ghosh , cite9†Arushi Goel , cite10†Chao-Han Huck Yang , cite11†Wenliang Dai , cite12†Zihan Liu , cite13†Hanrong Ye , cite14†Shinji Watanabe , cite15†Mohammad Shoeybi , cite16†Bryan Catanzaro , cite17†Rafael Valle , cite18†Wei Ping L12: View a PDF of the paper titled UALM: Unified Audio Language Model for Understanding, Generation and Reasoning, by Jinchuan Tian and 13 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite19†View PDF cite20†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Recent advances in the audio language modeling (ALM) domain tackle audio understanding and text-to-audio generation as separate tasks. Very few studies attempt to unify these tasks -- an essential step toward advanced multimodal reasoning. This paper introduces U}nified Audio Language Model (UALM), which aims to unify audio understanding, text-to-audio generation, and multimodal reasoning in a single model.\nL16: To achieve this goal, we first present UALM-Gen, a text-to-audio language model that directly predicts audio tokens and is comparable to state-of-the-art diffusion-based models. We then demonstrate, using proper data blending, training recipes, and inference techniques, that our single UALM model matches the quality of state-of-the-art specialized models in audio understanding, text-to-audio generation, and text reasoning.\nL17: Furthermore, we present UALM-Reason, a multimodal reasoning model that utilizes both text and audio in the intermediate thinking steps to facilitate complex generation tasks. To our knowledge, this is the first demonstration in audio research of cross-modal generative reasoning, with its effectiveness confirmed by subjective evaluations.\nL18: Subjects:  | Sound (cs.SD); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite21†arXiv:2510.12000 [cs.SD]\nL20:    | (or cite22†arXiv:2510.12000v1 [cs.SD] for this version)\nL21:    | cite23†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12000†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL22: ## Submission history\nL23: \nL24: From: Zhifeng Kong [cite24†view email ]\nL25: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 22:55:01 UTC (3,382 KB)\nL26: \nL27: Full-text links:\nL28: \nL29: ## Access Paper:\nL30: \nL31: View a PDF of the paper titled UALM: Unified Audio Language Model for Understanding, Generation and Reasoning, by Jinchuan Tian and 13 other authors\nL32: \nL33:   * cite19†View PDF L34:   * cite20†HTML (experimental) L35:   * cite25†TeX Source L36: \nL37: cite26†view license†creativecommons.org L38: ### Current browse context:\nL39: \nL40: cs.SD\nL41: \nL42: cite27†< prev |   cite28†next > L43: \nL44: cite29†new | cite30†recent | cite31†2025-10 L45: \nL46: Change to browse by:\nL47: \nL48: cite32†cs L49: cite33†cs.CL L50: cite34†cs.LG L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite35†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite36†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite37†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL74: \nL75: Bibliographic Explorer (cite38†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Connected Papers (cite39†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Litmaps (cite40†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL84